{"ts": 1789779253.719868, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** features **14 (long), 6 (long), 32 (short)** \u2014 in-sample annualized Sharpe **0.974**.\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe: 0.80 \u00b1 0.30**\n\n### Method\n\n1. **Screened all 40 features individually.** Most clustered near zero (|Sharpe| < 0.25). Two stood out clearly: feature 14 (0.704) and feature 6 (0.578), with a second tier of 32 (\u22120.404), 33 (\u22120.384), 36 (\u22120.353), 23 (0.342), 37 (0.341), 24 (\u22120.335).\n\n2. **Tested pairs among the top candidates,** orienting each by the sign of its standalone Sharpe. 14+6 dominated at 0.904 \u2014 a large gain over either alone, indicating the two carry substantially independent signal rather than duplicating each other.\n\n3. **Swept all 38 remaining features as a third leg** on the 14+6 base. Feature 32 (short) was best at 0.974. I also checked triples that drop 14 or 6 in case the pairwise-greedy path was misleading; none beat 0.86. Sign flips confirmed the orientation (reversing all three gives exactly \u22120.974, as expected).\n\n### On the uncertainty estimate\n\nThe 0.30 SD is deliberately wide, and the mean is shrunk ~0.17 below the in-sample figure. Two reasons:\n\n- **Selection bias.** I took the max over ~80 evaluations. With n=5000 daily observations the standard error on any single Sharpe is \u22480.22 annualized, which is large next to the gaps I was ranking on.\n- **A tight leaderboard.** The top four triples spanned 0.946\u20130.974. Differences that small are mostly noise, so the winner is partly the luckiest draw and should regress toward the group.\n\nOffsetting this, the components are individually robust \u2014 14 and 6 are the two strongest standalone features by a clear margin, and 32 is the strongest negative \u2014 so this isn't a spec assembled from noise, which caps how much shrinkage is warranted. The safer fallback is the 14+6 pair (0.904): feature 32 contributes ~0.07 in-sample, and I'd expect only about half that increment to persist out of sample."}
